WEBVTT

00:00.447 --> 00:01.886
- [Voiceover] We're told the graph of y

00:01.886 --> 00:03.650
equals two to the x is shown below,

00:03.650 --> 00:04.766
so that's the graph.

00:04.766 --> 00:06.205
It's an exponential function.

00:06.205 --> 00:07.748
Which of the following is a graph of y

00:07.748 --> 00:09.874
is equal to negative one times two

00:09.874 --> 00:13.170
to the x plus three plus four?

00:13.170 --> 00:16.223
They give us four choices down here.

00:16.223 --> 00:19.184
Before we even look
closely at those choices,

00:20.564 --> 00:22.817
let's just think about what this

00:22.817 --> 00:25.187
would look like if it was
transformed into that.

00:25.187 --> 00:27.264
You might notice that what we have here,

00:27.264 --> 00:29.179
this y that we wanna find the graph of,

00:29.179 --> 00:31.408
is a transformation of this original one.

00:31.408 --> 00:33.000
How do we transform it?

00:33.000 --> 00:35.345
We've replaced x with x plus three.

00:35.345 --> 00:37.760
Then we multiplied that by negative one,

00:37.760 --> 00:39.222
and then we add four.

00:39.222 --> 00:40.860
Let's take it step by step.

00:40.860 --> 00:42.682
This is y equals two to the x.

00:42.682 --> 00:45.202
What I wanna do next is let's graph y

00:45.202 --> 00:47.522
is equal to two to the x

00:47.522 --> 00:49.739
plus three power.

00:49.739 --> 00:52.281
If you replace x with x plus three,

00:52.281 --> 00:54.127
you're going to shift the graph

00:56.111 --> 00:58.352
to the left by three.

00:58.352 --> 01:00.594
That might be a little
bit counterintuitive,

01:00.594 --> 01:02.229
but when we actually
think about some points,

01:02.229 --> 01:04.087
it'll hopefully make some sense here.

01:04.087 --> 01:06.409
For example, over in our original graph,

01:06.409 --> 01:08.475
when x is equal to zero,

01:08.475 --> 01:10.750
y is equal to one.

01:13.061 --> 01:15.780
How do we get y equal
one for our new graph,

01:15.780 --> 01:17.441
for this thing right over here?

01:17.441 --> 01:20.076
To get y equals one
here, the exponent here

01:20.076 --> 01:22.119
still has to be zero, so
that's going to happen

01:22.119 --> 01:24.232
at x equals negative three.

01:24.232 --> 01:26.717
That's going to happen at
x equals negative three.

01:26.717 --> 01:28.620
Y is equal to one.

01:28.620 --> 01:31.873
Notice, we shifted to the left

01:31.873 --> 01:33.476
by three.

01:33.476 --> 01:35.115
Likewise, in our original graph,

01:35.115 --> 01:38.493
when x is two, y is four.

01:38.493 --> 01:41.511
How do we get y equals four in this thing

01:41.511 --> 01:43.009
right over here?

01:43.009 --> 01:46.664
For y to be equal to
four, this exponent here

01:46.664 --> 01:49.950
needs to be equal to two,
and so for this exponent

01:49.950 --> 01:53.338
to be equal to two, 'cause
two squared is four,

01:53.338 --> 01:54.895
for this exponent to be equal to two,

01:54.895 --> 01:57.100
x is going to be equal to negative one.

01:57.100 --> 01:59.712
When x is equal to negative one,

01:59.712 --> 02:01.523
y is equal to four.

02:01.523 --> 02:03.218
When x is equal to negative one,

02:03.218 --> 02:04.542
y is equal to four.

02:04.542 --> 02:07.023
Notice we shifted to the left

02:08.678 --> 02:10.140
by three.

02:10.140 --> 02:12.358
So this thing, which isn't our final graph

02:12.358 --> 02:14.308
that we're looking for, is gonna look

02:18.824 --> 02:20.745
something like,

02:20.745 --> 02:24.704
like that, which shifted,
it's y equals two to the x,

02:24.704 --> 02:27.065
shifted to the left by three.

02:27.065 --> 02:29.456
Now let's figure out what the graph of,

02:29.456 --> 02:31.766
now let's multiply this
expression times negative one.

02:31.766 --> 02:33.647
Notice we're slowly building up

02:33.647 --> 02:35.017
to our goal.

02:35.017 --> 02:36.642
Now let's figure out the graph of y

02:36.642 --> 02:39.056
is equal to negative one times two

02:39.056 --> 02:42.098
to the x plus three.

02:42.098 --> 02:44.745
Here, when y equals two
to the x plus three,

02:44.745 --> 02:46.417
if we multiply that times negative one,

02:46.417 --> 02:47.894
whatever y we had, we're gonna have

02:47.894 --> 02:49.298
the negative of that.

02:49.298 --> 02:50.726
Instead of when x is
equal to negative three

02:50.726 --> 02:52.537
having positive one, when
x equals negative three,

02:52.537 --> 02:53.733
you're gonna have negative one.

02:53.733 --> 02:55.474
We multiplied by negative one.

02:55.474 --> 02:57.576
When x is equal to negative
one, instead of having four,

02:57.576 --> 03:00.525
you're going to have negative four.

03:00.525 --> 03:03.241
Our graph is gonna be flipped over,

03:03.241 --> 03:06.318
it's flipped over the x axis.

03:06.318 --> 03:08.494
It's going to look something

03:10.984 --> 03:12.157
like this.

03:12.157 --> 03:14.130
This is not a perfect
drawing, but it'll give us

03:14.130 --> 03:15.605
a sense of things, and we can look

03:15.605 --> 03:18.182
at which of these graphs match up to that.

03:18.182 --> 03:22.048
Then finally, we wanna
add that four there.

03:22.048 --> 03:23.835
We wanna figure out the graph of y

03:23.835 --> 03:26.761
equals negative one times two to the x

03:26.761 --> 03:29.699
plus three plus four.

03:29.699 --> 03:31.358
We wanna take what we just had

03:31.358 --> 03:33.401
and shift it up by four.

03:33.401 --> 03:36.895
Instead of this being a
negative one right over here,

03:36.895 --> 03:40.413
this is going to be a negative one

03:40.413 --> 03:43.444
plus four is three.

03:43.444 --> 03:44.964
Instead of this being a negative four,

03:44.964 --> 03:48.644
negative four plus four is zero.

03:48.644 --> 03:50.687
Instead of our horizontal asymptote

03:50.687 --> 03:53.357
being at y equals zero,
our horizontal asymptote

03:53.357 --> 03:55.698
is going be at y equals four.

03:55.698 --> 03:58.322
It's gonna look like, let me draw,

03:58.322 --> 03:59.981
I can do a better job than that.

03:59.981 --> 04:01.456
Our horizontal asymptote is gonna

04:01.456 --> 04:03.522
be right over there, so our graph is going

04:03.522 --> 04:05.031
to look something like

04:08.565 --> 04:09.587
this.

04:09.587 --> 04:12.524
We just shifted that red graph up by four.

04:15.181 --> 04:16.563
Shifted it up by four.

04:16.563 --> 04:19.025
We have a horizontal
asymptote at y equals four.

04:19.025 --> 04:22.507
Let's look at which of
these choices match that.

04:22.507 --> 04:25.037
Choice A right over here
has a horizontal asymptote

04:25.037 --> 04:28.590
at y equals four, but it is shifted

04:28.590 --> 04:31.434
on the horizontal
direction inappropriately.

04:31.434 --> 04:32.990
In fact, it looks like it might have not

04:32.990 --> 04:35.033
been shifted to the left.

04:35.033 --> 04:36.972
We can rule this one out.

04:36.972 --> 04:38.969
Let's rule that one out.

04:38.969 --> 04:40.958
This one over here,

04:43.355 --> 04:46.792
this one approaches our
asymptote as x increases,

04:46.792 --> 04:48.812
so that's not right.

04:48.812 --> 04:50.287
It should approach our asymptote

04:50.287 --> 04:53.177
as x decreases, so we ruled that one out.

04:53.177 --> 04:57.100
Choice C looks like what we just graphed.

04:57.100 --> 05:00.270
Horizontal asymptote at x equals four.

05:00.270 --> 05:04.287
When x equals negative
three, y is equal to three.

05:04.287 --> 05:05.772
That's what we got.

05:05.772 --> 05:07.490
When x is equal to negative one,

05:07.490 --> 05:09.394
y is equal to zero.

05:09.394 --> 05:10.811
This looks right.

05:10.811 --> 05:13.435
You could even try those points out.

05:13.435 --> 05:15.582
We like choice C.

05:15.582 --> 05:17.741
D is clearly off.
